cloneElement is used when you want to pass some props to an existing element, but also want to keep the original props of that element. For example, if you have a component that is rendered by another component, and you want to add some new props to that component, but not overwrite the existing ones. In that case, you can use cloneElement to create a new element with the existing props and the new ones. Whereas, if you just pass the props directly, you would overwrite the existing props. So, cloneElement is useful when you want to add props without losing the existing ones.

Let me explain with an example. Suppose you have a component called Button, and you have another component called Wrapper that renders a Button. Now, if you want to add a new prop to the Button from the Wrapper component, you can use cloneElement to do that. For instance:

render() {
  return React.cloneElement(this.props.children, {
    color: 'red'
  });
}

In this case, the Button component will have the color prop set to red, but it will also retain any other props that were passed to it from its parent component. If instead, you just passed the props directly like this:

render() {
  return <Button {...this.props} color="red" />;
}

Then, if the Button component had other props, they would be overwritten by the props from this.props. So, cloneElement is useful when you want to add props without overwriting the existing ones.

